		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=3863#p3863"><![CDATA[
Your assumption that using conservative advancement will be slow due to iterations is not correct in my experience. So don't worry about that. As Dominic Mai <a href="http://cg.informatik.uni-freiburg.de/course_notes/WS06/ca_12_mai_coldet.pdf" class="postlink">points out</a> - it is usually just 4 iterations.<br><br>Both Gino and Erwin have written very clear papers on how to implement such a scheme. I recommend starting with them. You may also find useful information by searching on the term "conservative advancement".<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=60">dog</a> — Tue Mar 13, 2007 2:16 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
